  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **side length** of a square **decreases** at a rate **proportional** to its **area**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *side length $s$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *proportional to the area of the square*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-kA\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe side length of a square is given by \n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt{A}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{A}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{A}}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-kA \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{A}}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-kA \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2k{A}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=2k>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-K{A}^{3/2}.\n$$",
